<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kalachuris of Mahishmati</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 620 CE (approx.) · capital Mahishmati</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived but wide-ruling dynasty of western India whose silver coins circulated across the Deccan and whose era lasted for centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Early Kalachuris rose in the mid-6th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka dynasty</a> faded. Their kingdom centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahishmati">Mahishmati</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> and stretched over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, Malwa, the Konkan and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. They used the era of 248 later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalachuri-chedi-era">Kalachuri-Chedi era</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Three kings are known: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-kalachuri">Krishnaraja</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins circulated widely, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shankaragana">Shankaragana</a> and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddharaja">Buddharaja</a>. They were devotees of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupata-shaivism">Pashupata</a> tradition. Some historians attribute the great Shiva cave at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elephanta-caves">Elephanta</a> and early caves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora</a> to their patronage, though this is debated.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 600 to 610 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddharaja">Buddharaja</a> was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalesha">Mangalesha</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> completed the conquest of the Kalachuri lands. Later dynasties, notably the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalachuris-of-tripuri">Kalachuris of Tripuri</a>, claimed descent from them.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kalachuris-of-mahishmati.jpg" alt="Kalachuris of Mahishmati" width="320" height="153"><figcaption>Image: Rani nurmai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_coin_of_Krishnaraja_Kalachuri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 620 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mahishmati</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Krishnaraja, Shankaragana, Buddharaja</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shaivism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Early Kalachuris</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=585&amp;focus=kalachuris-of-mahishmati" aria-label="Kalachuris of Mahishmati on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6791 1367 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kalachuris of Mahishmati lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7097 1795h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7111 1623h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7111 1623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=585&amp;focus=kalachuris-of-mahishmati">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

